<TopicPage slug="subjects/entomology/foundational/insects-the-dominant-animals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    If dominance is measured by species, insects win without a contest: of roughly two million
    species of life humans have described, around one million are insects — about half of
    everything we have catalogued. Measured by individuals, the numbers leave the human scale
    behind entirely: a widely used estimate puts some ten quintillion insects alive at this
    moment, and a 2022 study concluded that ants alone number roughly twenty quadrillion
    individuals, with a total biomass exceeding that of all wild birds and wild mammals
    combined. Measured by ecological reach, the verdict is the same — insects pollinate most of
    the world's flowering plants (a large share of the food we eat), decompose most organic
    matter, form the base of countless food webs, and include the vectors of humanity's
    deadliest diseases. Whatever happens to the large, charismatic animals, Earth is and has
    been for 400 million years the planet of insects.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this happened by luck. Insect dominance is the result of a handful of engineering
    decisions embodied in a shared body plan: an external skeleton that is armour and water-seal
    in one; six legs plus a free pair of appendages that became mouthparts of astonishing
    variety; wings, invented before any vertebrate flew; a life cycle that can split an animal's
    youth from its adulthood; and a small body plan whose costs scale down gracefully. Each of
    these is visible in the anatomy — which is why entomology's first unit is anatomy, and why
    an entomologist reading a specimen can read its history: how it feeds, where it lives, what
    it was built to conquer.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter builds that foundation in three passes: what insects share with their arthropod
    relatives (the characters that define the phylum), how insects evolved and why they
    multiplied so successfully, and the external and internal anatomy that makes an insect an
    insect. Later units tour the major orders, metamorphosis, ecology and pest management —
    everything there depends on the vocabulary built here.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five inventions behind insect dominance" widget="equationStep" config={successCfg} />

  <h2>Arthropod characters: the shared body plan</h2>
  <p>
    Insects belong to the phylum <strong>Arthropoda</strong> — literally "jointed feet" — the
    largest phylum in the animal kingdom, which also contains spiders, crabs, centipedes and
    their kin. Five characters define every arthropod, and each is a solution to the same
    problem: how to build a complex animal out of segments. First, the body is
    <strong>segmented</strong>, with segments grouped into functional regions (a process called
    tagmosis) — head, thorax, abdomen in insects. Second, each segment primitively carries
    <strong>paired, jointed appendages</strong>, which evolution can modify freely: into legs,
    antennae, jaws, gills or reproductive claspers. Third, the whole animal is wrapped in an
    <strong>exoskeleton</strong> — a cuticle of the polysaccharide chitin stiffened with
    protein, serving as armour, muscle anchor and waterproofing in one structure. Fourth,
    because armour cannot stretch, arthropods must <strong>moult</strong>: grow by periodically
    shedding the cuticle (ecdysis), expanding briefly, and re-hardening. Fifth, arthropods have
    an <strong>open circulatory system</strong> — blood (haemolymph) washes freely through the
    body cavity rather than running in closed vessels — and a ventral nerve cord with paired
    segmental ganglia.
  </p>
  <p>
    Within that shared plan, the great arthropod groups are told apart by counting. Spiders,
    ticks, mites and scorpions (<strong>Chelicerata</strong>) have four pairs of walking legs,
    <em>no antennae</em>, and mouthparts called chelicerae; their body is merged into two
    regions. Crustaceans (crabs, shrimps, woodlice) carry <em>two pairs of antennae</em> and
    typically branched appendages, and remain mostly aquatic — modern molecular evidence makes
    them the insects' closest relatives, the two together forming the clade Pancrustacea.
    Myriapods (centipedes, millipedes) run on dozens to hundreds of legs, one or two pairs per
    trunk segment. And <strong>Hexapoda</strong> — the insects proper, plus a few small relatives
    like springtails — walk on <em>exactly six legs</em>, carry one pair of antennae, and divide
    the body into three clean sections. That six-legged, three-part blueprint is what this book
    studies, and the leg count is the fastest identification habit you can build: count legs,
    count antennae, and most misidentifications end.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a spider is not an insect, and neither is a tick, woodlouse
    or centipede. "Bug" is a household word, not a biological one — even in entomology it
    technically names only the true bugs (order Hemiptera). The arthropod phylum is a family
    tree with wildly different branches, and insects are one branch that turned out to be
    singularly good at terrestrial life.
  </p>

  <h2>Insect evolution and success</h2>
  <p>
    The timeline explains much of the success. Insects descended from crustacean-like ancestors
    and appear in the fossil record by roughly 400 million years ago, in the early Devonian.
    Their first world-changing move was <strong>flight</strong>: by the Carboniferous,
    about 350 million years ago, insects were flying — the first animals on Earth to do so,
    roughly 150 million years before pterosaurs, and the only invertebrates ever to manage
    powered flight. That Carboniferous air was rich in oxygen (perhaps 30 to 35 percent), which
    let giant forms like <em>Meganeura</em>, a dragonfly relative with a wingspan near 70
    centimetres, patrol coal swamps. Flight gave insects what no other invertebrate had: escape,
    dispersal, mate-finding across distance and access to food in the air itself — and once
    invented, it was never lost.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second move was <strong>coevolution with flowering plants</strong>. When angiosperms
    radiated through the Cretaceous (beginning roughly 130 million years ago), insects were
    waiting with the right equipment: wings to find flowers, antennae to smell them, and
    mouthparts pre-adapted to nectar. Pollination bound the two lineages together — plants
    evolved rewards and signals, insects evolved specialisations, and each radiation fed the
    other. The third move was <strong>metamorphosis</strong>. The lineage called Holometabola —
    beetles, flies, wasps and bees, butterflies and moths, and their relatives — evolved the
    complete life cycle, in which a worm-like larva dissolves in the pupa and rebuilds as an
    adult. Because larva and adult live in different worlds and eat different food, the young
    never compete with their own parents, and one genome exploits two niches. Holometabola
    includes roughly two-thirds of all insect species, and the largest order of all — the
    beetles, with some 350,000 to 400,000 described species — owes its dominance largely to
    this trick. (The biologist J.B.S. Haldane is said to have answered a cleric's question
    about what creation reveals with: "an inordinate fondness for beetles.")
  </p>
  <p>
    The fourth move is the least glamorous and the most fundamental: <strong>small body
    size</strong>. Insects live from a few hundred micrometres to a few centimetres long. Small
    bodies need little food and water, mature fast, reproduce in enormous numbers, hide in
    microhabitats — a rotting log is a continent at insect scale — and speciate easily as
    populations fragment. High reproduction is also extinction insurance: populations rebound
    from catastrophes that would end a species of large, slow-breeding animals. The result of
    all four moves compounded over 400 million years is the census with which this chapter
    opened: a million species described, several million more estimated, and success measured
    in quintillions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> evolution is not a ladder with humans at the top and insects
    below. Insects are not "primitive" — they are exquisitely <em>derived</em>, with sensory
    systems, flight machinery and life cycles far more specialised than ours. Their body plan
    solved the problems of terrestrial life differently from vertebrates, and for most of
    Earth's ecological niches, better.
  </p>

  <h2>External and internal anatomy</h2>
  <p>
    The insect body divides into three tagmata, each with its own story. The
    <strong>head</strong> is a sensory and feeding platform: a pair of large
    <strong>compound eyes</strong> built from hundreds to thousands of lens-tube units called
    ommatidia (excellent motion detection, and in bees the ability to see ultraviolet and
    polarised light), often supplemented by three simple <strong>ocelli</strong> for light
    sensing; one pair of antennae carrying smell and touch receptors of legendary sensitivity
    — male silkmoths can locate a female by a handful of pheromone molecules drifting on the
    wind. Below them sit the <strong>mouthparts</strong>: evolution's most instructive
    modification exercise. The ancestral chewing arrangement (toothed mandibles, maxillae, a
    labium) remains in beetles and grasshoppers; the same appendages have been re-engineered
    into the mosquito's piercing needle bundle, the butterfly's coiled siphoning proboscis, the
    housefly's sponge-tipped labellum, and the true bug's joint drinking-straw. One appendage
    set, a dozen professions — and the mouthpart type alone will usually tell you an insect's
    order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>thorax</strong> is the locomotor engine: three segments, each carrying a pair
    of legs, with the wing-bearing wings on the second and third. Six legs grant the division
    of labour that defines the class: three pairs stride in the insect's alternate-tripod gait
    while the mouthparts stay free — an arrangement that lets beetles walk and chew
    simultaneously. And legs themselves are modified by lifestyle: the grasshopper's enlarged
    saltatorial hind leg for jumping, the mole cricket's fossatorial digging claw, the mantis's
    raptorial grasping foreleg, the water strider's long, unwettable legs skating on the
    surface film. Wings
    come in pairs — two wings (flies, whose hind wings shrank into gyroscopic halteres) or four
    (beetles, whose forewings hardened into protective elytra over the flight wings beneath).
    The <strong>abdomen</strong> holds the visceral business: digestion, excretion,
    reproduction, and the paired breathing openings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Inside, everything runs on three systems that solve the same problems we solve, backwards.
    <strong>Gas exchange</strong> abandons lungs: air enters through valved
    <strong>spiracles</strong> along the body sides into a branching <strong>tracheal
    system</strong> of tubes that delivers oxygen directly to every tissue by diffusion. It is
    superbly efficient at small scale — and it is the reason insects cannot be big: tube
    length is diffusion-limited, so a dog-sized insect would suffocate at its core. (The
    Carboniferous giants flew under oxygen-rich air, and their disappearance tracks the oxygen
    decline.) <strong>Circulation</strong> is open: a dorsal vessel pumps haemolymph — which
    does <em>not</em> carry oxygen, the tracheae handle that — sloshing through the body cavity
    to bathe the organs. <strong>Excretion</strong> uses Malpighian tubules, which filter waste
    into uric acid, conserving water so beautifully that desert insects can live on metabolic
    water alone. The nervous system runs ventral, with a brain above the oesophagus and a chain
    of segmental ganglia below — which is why a headless cockroach can still walk, and why each
    segment keeps local control of its own reflexes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> insects do not breathe through their mouths, and their blood
    is not blood. Air enters through the side spiracles, never the mouth; and haemolymph is a
    clear or pale fluid that carries nutrients but no oxygen — a squashed insect does not bleed
    red because nothing in it binds oxygen for transport. Two of the most repeated "facts"
    about insects fail on the first diagram.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Flight is the field's engineering marvel.</strong> Large vertebrates power flight
    with massive pectoral muscles pulling the wing directly; insects evolved
    <strong>indirect flight muscles</strong> that deform the exoskeletal thorax, so the wing
    clicks between two positions like a snapping ruler — decoupling wingbeat frequency from
    nerve impulse rate. Asynchronous muscle systems let flies and bees beat their wings
    hundreds of times per second (mosquitoes approach 600 Hz, the source of their whine),
    while more primitive fliers like locusts and butterflies beat at tens of hertz with direct,
    impulse-per-stroke control. Halteres in flies act as vibrating gyroscopes feeding roll
    information to the flight controller — the first such sensor, a hundred million years
    before we built one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The exoskeleton's trade-offs organise the whole life history.</strong> Growth means
    moulting, and moulting means a soft, defenceless interphase and a body that cannot grow
    gradually — insects pass through discrete instars, steered by the hormones ecdysone (the
    moult trigger) and juvenile hormone (which decides what the next moult becomes). Critical
    size thresholds decide when an insect may metamorphose at all. Every aspect of the later
    unit on moulting and metamorphosis is downstream of the decision to wear your skeleton on
    the outside.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sensory biology sets the record for small-animal performance.</strong> Antennal
    chemoreception reaches a few molecules per second in some moths; bees navigate by
    polarised sky light and count landmarks; many insects hear with tympanal membranes on their
    legs or abdomen; and the compound eye trades acuity for temporal resolution — flicker
    fusion rates several times ours, which is why a fly watches your swatting hand move in
    slow motion. Miniaturisation has costs (small brains, limited pixels), but insect
    microglomeruli and vibration-sensitive hair sensilla show how much computation fits in a
    milligram.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions for the science.</strong> Species estimates still range from about
    five to ten million, and tropical canopies remain undersampled; global insect biomass
    trends are fiercely debated, with well-documented local declines alongside data-quality
    disputes — entomology's most consequential open problem, given that pollination and
    decomposition ride on the answer. How exactly wings evolved remains contested (from
    gill-like appendages? from outgrowths of the thorax?), and the genomic tree of the insect
    orders is still being refined. The dominant animals are, in short, still being discovered
    — and the anatomy in this chapter is the key that lets new species be read, identified and
    placed the moment they are found.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Triplehorn &amp; Johnson, <em>Borrer and DeLong's Introduction to the Study of Insects</em>; Gullan &amp; Cranston, <em>The Insects: An Outline of Entomology</em>.</p>

